            <ArticleTitle>Correlation between substance P content of primary sensory neurones and pain sensitivity in rats exposed to antibodies to nerve growth factor.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>351-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>An experimental autoimmune model was used to study effects of nerve growth factor deprivation on the peptide content of sensory neurones and on pain sensitivity. Rats exposed during development to anti-nerve growth factor antibodies showed an increased threshold towards nociceptive stimuli. These animals also showed a significant decrease in substance P levels in their dorsal root ganglia and spinal cord. These results suggest that substance P-containing primary sensory neurones are involved in certain types of nociception.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
